15:47:35 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: **Deconstructing the "Forex Guy" Myth: Removing Zeros vs. Economic Contraction** Okay, so the user is back with something they heard from those "forex guys on YouTube," and it's that dropping zeros will collapse the system because the currency is "too cheap and too high in supply." Right, I get it. I can see where this confusion is coming from. First, let's address the source of this information. YouTube is... well, it's a mixed bag. You have actual amateur traders, people just trying to get views, and, unfortunately, outright scammers. It's often a case of the blind leading the blind when it comes to sophisticated macroeconomic concepts. The core of their argument is that high supply combined with a low value somehow prevents a successful redenomination. This just isn't correct. Here's what's happening: they are confusing two completely different ideas. They're conflating *redenomination* (simply changing the face value of the currency by removing zeros) with *deflation or contraction* (actually removing money from the economy to artificially increase its value). * **Redenomination (Dropping Zeros):** This is just a proportional mathematical adjustment. It's like changing the units of measurement. If you have a currency that's very low in value, say 10,000 Dinar = $1, and you remove three zeros, you now have 10 New Dinar = $1. *Everything* is divided by the same factor: prices, wages, bank accounts, and the money supply. Think of it like a reverse stock split. If a company has 1 billion shares at $0.01, they can do a 1-for-100 reverse split. Now they have 10 million shares at $1.00. The company didn't collapse, right? The *real* value stays the same. The overall market cap, or value, doesn't change. * **Deflation/Contraction (What They're *Actually* Describing):** *This* is what the YouTube guys are talking about, but calling it by the wrong name. If a government tried to make its currency more valuable by literally destroying 99% of the money supply without adjusting wages, prices, or debt, *that* would be catastrophic. The country would grind to a halt because there wouldn't be enough liquidity. Regarding the "too high in supply" part: if you have 10 trillion old Dinar and drop three zeros, you now have 10 billion new Dinar. The *nominal* supply drops, but the *real* value of the supply stays exactly the same when all values are redenominated. It's all about proportionality. In essence, these "forex guys" are misunderstanding a basic mathematical concept. It's important to recognize that, while some retail forex traders are savvy, many are not macroeconomists. They trade charts and don't spend their time studying central bank mechanics. It’s like believing a plumber knows the right thing to do with your computer.
15:47:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The reason those YouTube forex traders are saying this is because they are confusing two completely different economic concepts: **Redenomination** (changing the math) versus **Deflationary Contraction** (destroying actual wealth/liquidity). When they say "removing zeros will collapse the system because the supply is too high," they are describing the second concept, not the first. Here is exactly where those YouTubers are getting their wires crossed, and why it is not a real structural limit. ### The Mix-Up: "Dropping Zeros" vs. "Burning Money" **1. What the YouTubers are thinking of (Burning Money / Contraction)** Imagine Iraq has 100 Trillion Dinar in circulation. If the government suddenly said, *"We are going to literally destroy 99% of the money supply to make the remaining Dinar rare and valuable,"* **that** would collapse the system. * There would be no cash left for businesses to pay employees. * Banks would freeze. * People wouldn't be able to buy food. If you try to remove the supply of money *without* changing the prices of goods, the economy grinds to a halt. This is what the YouTubers mean when they say "the supply is too high to remove it." **2. What dropping zeros actually is (Redenomination)** But that is **not** how countries drop zeros. When a country drops zeros, they aren't just taking money away; they are dividing *everything in the entire economy* by the exact same number at the exact same time. * The 100 Trillion Dinar money supply becomes 100 Billion "New Dinar." * A citizen's bank account goes from 1,000,000 Dinar to 1,000 "New Dinar." * The price of a loaf of bread goes from 1,000 Dinar to 1 "New Dinar." Because the money supply, the wages, and the prices all drop by the exact same percentage, **purchasing power remains identical.** There is no shock to the system. The system doesn't collapse because nothing actually changed except the ink on the paper. ### The "Reverse Stock Split" Analogy Since these are forex traders, the best way to understand this is to look at the stock market. Imagine a company has 1 Billion shares of stock, and each share is worth $0.01 (a penny stock). The supply is massive, and the price is cheap. If the company wants to get rid of the zeros, they do a **Reverse Stock Split** (usually 1-for-100). * They take back the 1 Billion shares and issue 10 Million new shares. * The price per share goes from $0.01 to $1.00. * If you owned 10,000 shares worth $100, you now own 100 shares... still worth exactly $100. Did the company collapse because the "supply was too high and too cheap"? No. The total value of the company (Market Cap) stayed exactly the same. Dropping zeros from a currency is just a Reverse Stock Split for a country. The central bank issues 1 "New Dinar" for every 1,000 "Old Dinar." The supply shrinks mathematically, the value of a single bill rises mathematically, but the total wealth of the country does not change by a single penny. ### Why Forex YouTubers get this wrong It is important to understand that retail Forex trading and Macroeconomics are two very different skill sets. Many Forex YouTubers are technical analysts—they study charts, momentum, and trading algorithms to make short-term profits. They are not central bankers or monetary economists. They look at a currency with a massive supply (like the Vietnamese Dong) and view it through the lens of a trader: *"If you try to buy up all this supply to drive the price up, you'll run out of money and crash the market."* They are absolutely right about that! A country cannot *buy up* its own massive supply to force the value up. But a sovereign government doesn't have to buy its own money to drop zeros. They just pass a law, print new bills, and declare a 1,000-to-1 exchange rate for the new cash. **The Bottom Line:** High supply and low value do not prevent a country from dropping zeros. It actually makes them the *perfect* candidate for it. If you divide a massive supply by 1,000, and divide all the prices by 1,000, the economy continues functioning the exact same way it did yesterday—just with less zeros on the calculator.
